Note that:

```C++
if (p_unassign_id) {
	body_iface.UnassignBodyID(jolt_body->GetID());
	body_iface.DestroyBodyWithoutID(jolt_body);
} else {
	body_iface.DestroyBody(jolt_body->GetID());
}
```

Is placebo because both the `if` and `else` path are identical in functionality.

> CreateBodyWithID is called right after, so there shouldn't be any opportunity for any other body to claim it, unless I'm misunderstanding something.

Technically that would work because UnassignBodyID pushes the BodyID to the front of the free list, so you get it the next time you create a body. I wouldn't rely on getting the first freed ID as that could be something I change in the future. Also, the sequence number of the BodyID will be incremented, so if you compare the entire ID they would still mismatch.

> It was mostly just a way to be able to still use jolt_settings without having to manually assign its properties over to the recycled body.

In that case, maybe I should add a specific helper function for that.

> I assume this means that if you pull out some massive level branch out of the scene tree, containing half of the max bodies amount, and then add another massive level branch to the scene tree, containing another half of the max bodies amount, you'll end up hitting the limit and trigger the error, despite seemingly keeping under the limit.

Correct, although you could also start destructing bodies from the `SelfList` if you run out of bodies (with as an obscure side effect that you reintroduce the possibilty for this bug in that specific context).
